node-addon-api installs the C++ wrapper headers (napi.h, napi-inl.h and
napi-inl.deprecated.h) for an engine that embeds this repository's Node.js.

    find_package(unofficial-node-addon-api CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(your_addon PRIVATE unofficial::node-addon-api::node-addon-api)

The target carries this port's include directory. <node_api.h> is provided by
unofficial::node::node, which this port depends on; link that target as well, and do not add
another node_api.h to the include path.

Scope: this is an overlay, not the registry port. It depends on the node port, so it builds
Node.js and compiles the addon against that tree's headers, and it supports the node port's
six cells including arm64 Windows. That is right for an addon linked into an embedding
executable. A project that builds a .node module for an installed node.exe should use the
registry port instead and should not put this overlay on its port path.
